{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And his mother's name.</b> Whoever shows signs of brazenness and unworthiness, it is fitting to investigate after his mother, for there is no doubt that some admixture of impurity became mixed into that drop, whether in thought or in deed. And it would have been fitting for Scripture to say, 'And there went out the son of an Israelite woman, and her name was Shelomit daughter of Dibri, of the tribe of Dan'; but it did not wish to mention her name until it mentioned the sin of the son — that is, 'and he blasphemed the Name and cursed' — and afterward it said 'and his mother's name was,' to make clear that she was the cause of the sin and the corruption. And the reason for this is that the fetus is formed from menstrual blood and is nourished in his mother's womb for a fixed time; also after birth and his emergence into the air of the world, he nurses from his mother's breasts. Therefore it follows that his nature is bound to the nature of the mother more than to the nature of the father, and his deeds resemble his mother's deeds more than they resemble his father's deeds. From this, Scripture mentioned the names of the mothers of the kings of Israel, as it says (II Chronicles 20:31) ״ושם אמו עזובה בת שלחי״, and (I Kings 15:2) ושם אמו מעכה בת אבישלום״ — to make clear that a person's nature resembles his mother's nature and follows after it. And the same applies to the good: for when a person is righteous and humble and a man of good traits, this is a testimony about his mother that she was modest and of good thought, and therefore she merited him, for a good branch testifies to the quality of the root. And this is what is written (Exodus 6:20): ״ויקח עמרם את יוכבד דודתו לו לאשה״ — Scripture mentioned the righteous man who married a righteous woman, and righteous ones emerged from between them, as it is written (ibid.): ״ותלד לו את אהרן ואת משה ואת מרים אחותם״. And so Scripture mentioned (Exodus 6:25): ״ואלעזר בן אהרן לקח לו מבנות פוטיאל״ — for Elazar took from the daughters of Putiel, who was of the seed of Joseph the righteous, and a righteous one emerged from between them, and that is what is written immediately: ״ותלד לו את פינחס״. And so we find with King David, peace be upon him, who said (Psalms 116:16): ״אני עבדך בן אמתך״. From the fact that Scripture publicized her name, we learn the praise of Israel, that there was among them no harlot except this one alone. And do not wonder and say, how could she have been a harlot when she thought he was her husband? It has already been explained that a harlot by Torah law is any woman who had relations with one forbidden to her." "<b>Shelomit</b> — who used to greet everyone with peace, chattering and saying, 'Peace upon you, peace upon you.' <b>Daughter of Dibri, of the tribe of Dan</b> — this tells that the wicked person causes disgrace to his father and to his mother and disgrace to his tribe. Likewise, the righteous person brings praise and greatness to himself, to his father, and to his tribe, as it is written (Exodus 38:23): ״ואתו אהליאב בן אחיסמך למטה דן״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:09.472681663Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}